---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Icon from "../../components/Icon.astro";
import { ICON_SIZES } from "../../data/iconSizes";

const title = "Request Live Music — Derek DiLeo";
const description =
  "How a song-request idea became the project that started Derek DiLeo’s software career.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <main class="case-study container" id="top">
    <a class="back-link" href="/#projects">
      <Icon name="arrow" size={ICON_SIZES.sm} /> Back to selected work
    </a>

    <header class="case-study__hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Origin story · Flutter · Dart · Firebase</p>
      <h1>An idea that started a career.</h1>
      <p class="case-study__intro">
        Request Live Music began with a problem I experienced behind the booth:
        trying to understand a song request while counting beats, reading the
        room, and planning the next few tracks. It became my first real product,
        my first production crash, and the project that convinced me I could
        become a developer.
      </p>
      <div class="case-study__meta">
        <span>Role</span>
        <strong>Product builder</strong>
        <span>Timeline</span>
        <strong>2019–2022</strong>
        <span>Source</span>
        <a
          href="https://github.com/derekdotdev/request-live-mobile"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          GitHub <Icon name="arrow" size={ICON_SIZES.xs} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-study__body">
      <aside class="case-study__aside">
        <p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Users, first night</dt><dd>10</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Requests, first night</dt><dd>70+</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Original backend</dt><dd>Rails</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Mobile rebuild</dt><dd>Flutter</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </aside>

      <article class="case-study__prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">The beginning</p>
        <h2>An idea with a real audience.</h2>
        <p>
          I was playing music at a bar and needed a better way to receive song
          requests. A DJ is constantly counting beats, reading the room, and
          preparing the next few songs based on how the crowd is responding.
          Then someone might tap you on the shoulder and try to communicate a
          request over the music—sometimes after a few drinks, sometimes across
          a language barrier, and almost always while you were in the middle of
          something else.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nobody was doing anything wrong. The environment simply made a precise
          conversation difficult. I wanted a way for someone to submit an
          artist, title, and note from their phone, then give the entertainer a
          clear request list to work from.
        </p>
        <p>
          My friend Max—a former DJ who had become a developer—agreed to help me
          build the first version, but only if I pair-programmed with him. He
          saw something in me before I saw it in myself, and that decision
          changed the direction of my life.
        </p>

        <p class="eyebrow">The first production test</p>
        <h2>Ten users. Seventy requests. One crash.</h2>
        <p>
          Our first version was a Ruby on Rails web app. I played music videos
          at the bar and streamed the request site’s URL across the screens so
          people could participate while I was DJing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Authentication supported Google and Facebook OAuth2 sign-in as well as
          email registration and login. Once authenticated, a listener could
          submit an artist, title, and note, then receive confirmation that the
          request had been received.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the first night, people submitted more than seventy requests. I was
          thrilled—and then the app crashed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before reading the error message, I already knew what had happened. We
          generated user thumbnails from their Google and Facebook profile
          images, but one of our users had registered with email. That account
          had no social profile image to fall back on.
        </p>
        <p>
          It was a small bug, but recognizing it before opening the logs was a
          major moment for me. When I told Max about the night, including the
          success and the failure, he told me I would one day make a great
          developer.
        </p>

        <p class="eyebrow">The rebuild</p>
        <h2>From working prototype to multiplatform experiment.</h2>
        <p>
          At the time, I was studying Java in college and had not yet
          encountered Kotlin or Jetpack Compose. I wanted to build a mobile
          version for multiple platforms, and Flutter offered the most
          approachable path to a shared codebase. I learned Dart and built the
          app myself.
        </p>
        <p>
          Riverpod was my first real experience with dependency injection. It
          felt like magic: not every dependency had to be passed down through
          every parent and child. Some dependencies could simply be available
          where they were needed. That experience taught me to recognize when
          shared behavior deserves an explicit dependency boundary.
        </p>
        <p>
          Flutter also introduced me to the difference between stateful and
          stateless UI components. I was learning to decide which pieces of a
          screen should own state and which should simply render the state they
          received. That idea maps directly to how I approach Jetpack Compose
          today: keep state ownership deliberate, hoist it when it belongs
          elsewhere, and make reusable components predictable.
        </p>
        <p>
          The rebuild also used Firebase and Cloud Firestore for the backend
          services and request data. Working with a hosted backend let me focus
          on the product experience while learning how authentication, shared
          state, and real-time data fit together across a multiplatform app.
        </p>
        <p>
          I imagined a product where listeners could use the service for free
          with advertising, while entertainers could subscribe for access to
          richer tools: tips through services like Venmo or Cash App, direct
          messaging, social links, entertainer follows, and scheduled events. A
          couple could submit wedding requests ahead of time so the DJ could
          prepare a complete library before the event.
        </p>

        <p class="eyebrow">What it taught me</p>
        <h2>Build the idea. Then learn what it needs next.</h2>
        <p>
          The Flutter app had no meaningful test suite, mocks, or fakes. It did
          not follow Clean Architecture, and the UI was designed by instinct
          rather than a system. Eventually I reached the point where I could no
          longer add value without bringing more experienced developers into the
          project.
        </p>
        <p>
          At the time, that felt like a limit. In retrospect, it was a useful
          boundary. I had taken an idea from a conversation to a live product,
          learned a new language and framework, and found the edge of my own
          capabilities by building toward it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Today, I would approach the same product with explicit boundaries,
          automated tests, resilient state management, and a more deliberate UI
          architecture. That is not a reason to dismiss the original work. It is
          the reason I know how much I have grown.
        </p>
        <p>
          Max is no longer here to see where that encouragement led, but I still
          think about his confidence in me whenever I take on something
          difficult. I’m still trying to live up to it.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .case-study {
    padding-top: 10rem;
    padding-bottom: 7rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .back-link svg {
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }

  .case-study__hero {
    max-width: 60rem;
    margin: 5rem 0 4rem;
  }
  h1 {
    max-width: 10ch;
    margin: 1rem 0 1.75rem;
    font-size: clamp(3.5rem, 9vw, 8rem);
    line-height: 0.92;
    letter-spacing: -0.07em;
  }
  .case-study__intro {
    max-width: 38rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .case-study__meta {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, max-content);
    gap: 0.5rem 1.2rem;
    margin-top: 3rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font: 0.68rem/1.3 var(--font-mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .case-study__meta strong,
  .case-study__meta a {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .case-study__body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(12rem, 0.6fr) minmax(0, 1.4fr);
    gap: clamp(3rem, 10vw, 9rem);
    margin-top: 7rem;
  }
  .case-study__aside {
    position: sticky;
    top: 8rem;
    align-self: start;
  }
  dl {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
  }
  dl div {
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  }
  dt {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font: 0.62rem/1.3 var(--font-mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  .case-study__prose {
    max-width: 42rem;
  }
  .case-study__prose .eyebrow:not(:first-child) {
    margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  .case-study__prose h2 {
    max-width: 15ch;
    margin: 0.8rem 0 1.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.055em;
  }
  .case-study__prose p:not(.eyebrow) {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .case-study {
      padding-top: 8rem;
    }
    .case-study__hero {
      margin-top: 4rem;
    }
    .case-study__meta {
      grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    }
    .case-study__body {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      margin-top: 5rem;
    }
    .case-study__aside {
      position: static;
    }
  }
</style>
